## 5. End-to-end tests (Playwright, against a seeded `_test` database)
Create a global setup that seeds a known dataset and tears down only what it created. Keep each flow independent. Write tests for:
1. Public: home loads, search finds a seeded lecturer and a seeded course, lecturer page renders, event page renders, news page renders.
2. Alumni: submit with two programmes and consent, see the thank-you, open the confirmation link, see the people-you-might-know list; admin approves; the alumnus appears on /alumni; an unapproved one does not.
3. Journal: author registers, submits a manuscript (upload a small DOCX), receives a reference number; EiC assigns an editor and a reviewer; reviewer accepts through the invitation link and submits a review; editor records a decision; author revises; editor accepts; EiC prepares and publishes; the article page, citation export and RSS entry exist; retraction leaves the page with a notice. Assert that the author never sees reviewer identity and the reviewer never sees the title page.
4. Access: HOD and ADMIN get 403 on journal editorial pages, HOD can read manuscripts read-only, unauthenticated users are redirected to login.
5. Content admin: HOD edits a news item and a lecturer field and the public page updates.
These must run in CI-friendly mode (`npx playwright test`), with traces on failure. Run the whole suite twice and report both.

